INT. DRIVE-IN PROJECTION BOOTH - NIGHT

Cramped. Dust in the lamp beam. A reel spins on its own. Through the slit window: an empty lot, a flickering screen.

NELL AUGUSTINE, 20s, the projectionist, stands rigid. Beside her, her brother SULLY, 16, holds a flashlight with both hands. DOROTHEA "DOT" FEENEY, 60s, the owner, is wedged against the film cabinet, mouthing a prayer. At the door, MR. KWABENA ASARE, 50s, a customer with a ruined suit, keeps one shoulder to the wood while a hand slams from the other side. At the window, the booth's cat-eyed night guard BRYCE, 30s, whips the curtain shut, but a pale arm slides through the vent and clamps his ankle, dragging him down.

BRYCE
Dot! Dot, pull!

DOT
I'm pulling, Bryce, I'm pulling!

Sully swings the flashlight onto the arm. Under the light, the skin peels like old film.

SULLY
Nell, it's on the reel!

NELL
Don't look at the screen!

Kwabena braces. The door cracks. Nell grabs the lamp housing and tears it open. White light blasts into the vent. The arm SCREAMS and lets go.

Silence. The reel keeps spinning.

KWABENA
(panting)
Is it gone?

On the screen, in huge flickering black and white, something turns toward the camera.
